#use(std/code)

#section{Command line}::

Render a document with the bundled CLI (or #code{nix run . --} in place of
#code{racket -l- yamd/cli}):

#code(lang: "plain")
	racket -l- yamd/cli doc.yamd                      # HTML to stdout
	racket -l- yamd/cli doc.yamd --backend html -o doc.html
	racket -l- yamd/cli index.yamd --mode multi -o out/
	nix run . -- doc.yamd --backend typst -o doc.typ

Flags:

#list
	#item: #code{--backend html|markdown|typst} — output serialization (default #code{html}); see the backends page.
	#item: #code{--mode single|multi} — build strategy (default #code{single}). #code{single} produces one output (toctree children inlined, headings shifted); #code{multi} requires #code{-o <directory>} and writes one page per source document, subdirectories preserved, with prev/up/next navigation and cross-page links rewritten.
	#item: #code{-o out} — output file (single) or directory (multi); default stdout.
	#item: #code{--fmt [--check]} — the source formatter; see the formatting page.

A document may instead be a Racket module by starting with #code{#lang yamd}:

#code(lang: "plain")
	#lang yamd
	#section{Hello}::

	Some #emph{emphasis}.

Such a module provides #code{doc} — the resolved document Content, for
programmatic export — and running it directly (#code{racket file.rkt})
prints the rendered HTML via its #code{main} submodule. #code{#use},
plugins, contexts and two-pass behave identically to the CLI pipeline: the
module re-runs the same pipeline at instantiation (it is #strong{not}
compiled to Racket code).

From Racket, the same pipeline is a library:

#code(lang: "racket")
	(require yamd)
	(display (yamd-file->html "doc.yamd"))
	(display (yamd-string->html "#emph{hello}"))

Corner cases:

#list
	#item: Flags may follow the input file; parsing is permuted on purpose.
	#item: Unknown backends and missing input files fail with a clean #code{yamd: …} message and exit 1, no stack trace.
	#item: Backend modules are loaded only when selected — an unselected backend need not even exist.
	#item: The CLI always routes through the doc-set builder, so toctree documents work; the plain #code{yamd-file->html} API errors on them.
